\description{
Fits the CAMS model (Panaro, Röver & Friede 2025) for \strong{consistent
inference on treatment effect modification} from modifier-level
aggregate data (vignettes often use classic \strong{subgroup} tables).
Modifier-level treatment effects are inputs and derived outputs; the
methodological target is not a separate meta-analysis within each modifier level.
}
\details{
\code{\link[=cams]{cams()}} is the fitter.

By default, \code{\link[=ifrac]{ifrac()}} is run inside the fitter on the input (e.g. a
\code{\link[metafor:escalc]{metafor::escalc}} object). You can also pass an existing \code{\link[=ifrac]{ifrac()}} tibble.

For two subgroups per trial, CAMS fits an information-fraction
meta-regression of combined coordinates \eqn{m_j} and a random-effects
meta-analysis of within-trial contrasts \eqn{g_j} (Panaro, Röver &
Friede 2025, §3.4). Default inference is \strong{Bayesian} (\strong{bayesmeta});
\strong{\code{method = "REML"}} (or other \strong{\code{metafor::rma()}} methods) gives
frequentist inference.
}
\section{Sampling assumption (within-trial covariance)}{

Within each trial,
\eqn{g_j=y_{Bj}-y_{Aj}} with \eqn{\mathrm{Var}(g_j)=v_{Aj}+v_{Bj}} and
information fraction
\eqn{\pi_j=(1/v_{Bj})/(1/v_{Aj}+1/v_{Bj})} (IV weight of the non-reference
subgroup). That choice makes \eqn{\mathrm{Cov}(m_j,g_j)=0} when
\eqn{\mathrm{Cov}(y_{Aj},y_{Bj})=0}. This package currently assumes that
within-trial subgroup sampling covariance is zero. Separate overall and
interaction fits (\code{bmr}/\code{bayesmeta} or two \code{rma}s) rely on that
orthogonality. If subgroup estimates share a control arm or other
within-trial dependence, both \eqn{\mathrm{Var}(g_j)} and separate
fits for \eqn{m_j} and \eqn{g_j} are misspecified unless \eqn{\pi_j} and the variances are
redefined with the covariance (not implemented here).
}

\section{Model}{

\code{\link[=cams]{cams()}} fits the \strong{comparison} CAMS model with free ecological /
composition slope \eqn{\beta} (\code{b_ifrac}): population lines
\eqn{\mu_A=\alpha}, \eqn{\mu_B=\alpha+\gamma+\beta}, \eqn{M(\pi)=\alpha+\pi\beta+\gamma}.
}

\section{Inference}{

CAMS can be fitted using Bayesian or frequentist inference. These are not
two different scientific models. Set \strong{\code{method = "Bayesian"}} (default) for
\strong{bayesmeta}, or any \strong{\code{metafor::rma()}} \code{method} for the two
frequentist components (default frequentist choice is \strong{\code{"REML"}}). See
\code{vignette("cams")}.

For Bayesian fits, bayesmeta options may go in \code{fit_args} or \code{...}.
For metafor fits, further \strong{\code{rma()}} arguments (e.g. \code{test}, \code{control}) may
be passed in \code{...} or \code{fit_args} (\code{fit_args} wins on conflicts).

\code{\link[=swada]{swada()}}, \code{\link[=da]{da()}}, and \code{\link[=ad]{ad()}} are \strong{not} alternative CAMS models. SWADA
does not use the CAMS information-fraction (IF) construction; IF
quantities and IF-based plots are not available for SWADA objects.
Comparison helpers may combine multiple user-supplied fits only when
those fits are passed explicitly.
}

\section{Related analyses}{

CAMS is the \strong{statistical model}. \code{\link[=da]{da()}} (Difference of Averages) and
\code{\link[=ad]{ad()}} (Average Difference) are \strong{different constructions} (Panaro,
Röver & Friede 2026) for a treatment-by-subgroup interaction from
subgroup-level aggregate data. They can use different study weights and
need not agree. \code{\link[=swada]{swada()}} applies the SWADA \emph{weighting principle} so
those analyses share one contribution vector; under the default
Interaction RE-weights, the DA interaction equals AD. \code{\link[=conventional]{conventional()}}
is a compatibility bundle of DA and AD. None of these is the 2002 van
Houwelingen joint bivariate mixed model.
}

\section{Method}{

\strong{\code{method}} selects inference: \code{"Bayesian"} uses bayesmeta; values documented
for \code{\link[metafor:rma]{metafor::rma()}} (\code{"REML"}, \code{"ML"}, \code{"DL"}, \code{"PM"}, …) use metafor on
both components.
The fit stores \code{fit$method} (e.g. \code{"Bayesian"}, \code{"REML"}), \code{fit$inference},
and \code{fit$support} (DIRECT quadrature support or one-row metafor estimate).
\code{\link[=print.interactmeta]{print.interactmeta()}} shows the same wording as
the \code{cams(..., method = )} argument (\code{Bayesian}, \code{REML}, …).
}

\section{Priors}{

Bayesian CAMS passes priors through to bayesmeta. They are stored on
\code{fit$priors}. \strong{Heterogeneity defaults match \code{\link[bayesmeta:bayesmeta]{bayesmeta::bayesmeta()}}}:
\describe{
\item{fixed effects}{independent \eqn{\mathrm{Normal}(0, 10)} on the
overall-block means \eqn{(\alpha,\theta)} and on the interaction mean
\eqn{\gamma}, with \eqn{\theta=\beta+\gamma}. Stored \code{b_ifrac} is the
reconstructed ecological slope \eqn{\beta=\theta-\gamma}; that prior is
\strong{not} an independent \eqn{N(0,10)} on \eqn{\beta}. (bayesmeta's
improper flat \eqn{\mu} is not the CAMS default: the overall block is
fit with \code{bmr}, where a proper normal is the reliable companion to
improper tau)}
\item{\code{tau.prior}}{improper uniform \eqn{p(\tau_m)\propto 1}
(\eqn{\tau_m \ge 0}), bayesmeta's \code{tau.prior = "uniform"}}
\item{\code{tau.interaction.prior}}{the same improper uniform on
\eqn{\tau_g} when both heterogeneity priors are omitted. If
\code{tau.interaction.prior} is \code{NULL} and the user supplied
\code{tau.prior}, the interaction prior \strong{inherits} that density}
}
Character shorthands \code{"uniform"} and \code{"sqrt"} are the \strong{improper} bayesmeta
densities \eqn{p(\tau)\propto 1} and \eqn{p(\tau)\propto \tau^{-1/2}}
(\eqn{\tau \ge 0}). They are \strong{not} \eqn{U(0, U)}.
Frequentist \code{method} values ignore Bayesian priors (warning if supplied).

\strong{Empirical heterogeneity (not the package default).} CDSR calibration
suggests interaction heterogeneity is often substantially smaller than
overall treatment-effect heterogeneity, so a common weakly informative
half-normal on both blocks can be too diffuse for \eqn{\tau_g}
(Panaro, Röver & Friede, arXiv:2606.23968). That paper reports
measure-specific empirical scales (e.g. \eqn{\mathrm{HN}(0.5)}-style WIP
for overall log-ratio / SMD-type endpoints in Röver et al., 2021;
tighter interaction scales such as \eqn{\mathrm{HN}(0.1)} in sensitivity
analyses and \eqn{\mathrm{HN}(0.07)} empirically for log-ROR). Vignettes
set those half-normal densities \strong{explicitly}; they are not silently
applied by \code{\link[=cams]{cams()}}.

\strong{Fixed-effect parameterization:} bayesmeta/bmr receive independent
\eqn{N(0, 10)} on \eqn{(\alpha,\theta)} (\code{beta.prior.cov = diag(sd^2)}) and
\code{mu.prior} on \eqn{\gamma}. That prior factors across the two orthogonal
blocks. Independent normals on \eqn{(\alpha,\beta,\gamma)} would couple
\eqn{\theta} and \eqn{\gamma} a priori and are not what this engine uses.
}

\section{Intervals}{

For Bayesian CAMS, public extractors (\code{\link[=modifiers]{modifiers()}}, \code{\link[=interactions]{interactions()}},
\code{\link[=combined]{combined()}}, \code{\link[=predict_cams_by_if]{predict_cams_by_if()}}, \code{\link[=shrinkage]{shrinkage()}}) report credible
intervals from the DIRECT-node Gaussian mixture (\code{.weight} and within-node
\verb{.cond_*} moments). Default \code{interval.type} is \code{"central"} (equal-tailed);
pass \code{interval.type = "shortest"} to \code{\link[=cams]{cams()}} for the narrowest interval rule.
Default \code{\link[=combined]{combined()}} and \code{\link[=interactions]{interactions()}} at \code{type = "Overall"} with no
\code{exponentiate} use stored \code{bmr} / \code{bayesmeta} intervals when possible;
other requests still use the mixture grid.
\code{\link[=plot_contribution]{plot_contribution()}} uses central (equal-tailed) mixture intervals for
ribbon limits by default (\code{ribbon_interval.type}). Alpha and theta are never
convolved with each other.
}

